Here is an example C++ program that finds the nth Fibonacci number using recursion. (Note: n is the number of elements in the Fibonacci sequence)

```c++
#include <iostream>

int Fibonacci(int n)
{
    // The 'base case' is when we get to the first 2 Fibonacci numbers
    if (n <= 2)
        return 1;

    // We each have a recursive call, with n-1 as the argument, which
    // represents the Fibonacci number for the 'prev' two (n-2) numbers
    return Fibonacci(n - 1) + Fibonacci(n - 2);
}

int main()
{
    int n = 0; // Variable to hold the number of elements of the Fibonacci sequence
    std::cout << "Enter the number of elements in the Fibonacci sequence : ";
    std::cin >> n;

    // Debugging code
    if (n < 0 || n > iterMax)
        std::cout << "Error: The number of Fibonacci numbers is too large." << std::endl;

    int factorialNumber = 1; // Stores the nth Fibonacci number

    // Recursion stops when 'n' is less than 2 (base case)
    while (n > 1)
    {
        factorialNumber *= n; // Multiplying n by n for each recursive call
        n--; // Decrement for each recursive call
    }

    std::cout << "Nth Fibonacci number : " << factorialNumber << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

You can replace iterMax constant with the largest number you want to calculate recursively so if you enter a number less than zero or greater than iterMax, there will be no memoization and you'll only get the correct answer.

